<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#hallstatt-culture">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hallstatt culture</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 800–450 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> · Also known as Hallstatt period</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hallstatt culture was the main archaeological culture of western and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, from about 800 to 450 BCE, and is commonly associated with speakers of Proto-Celtic. It grew out of the Urnfield culture and is named after Hallstatt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, where Johann Georg Ramsauer found a cemetery in 1846 and the salt mines preserved textiles, wood and leather. Towards its end people of high rank were buried under mounds with Greek imports, as at Vix and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hochdorf-chieftains-grave">Hochdorf</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture</a> followed in much of its area.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hallstatt-culture.jpg" alt="Hallstatt culture" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dbachmann · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hallstatt_LaTene.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Hallstatt culture developed out of the Urnfield culture and was followed in much of its area by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture</a>. Paul Reinecke's scheme of 1902, based on finds from the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, divided the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and the Early Iron Age into four periods, Hallstatt A to D. Hallstatt A and B are regarded as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>, while Hallstatt C and D, dated roughly 800 to 620 BCE and 620 to 450 BCE, make up the Early Iron Age phase. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radiocarbon-dating">Radiocarbon dating</a> is hard to use here because of the so-called Hallstatt Plateau, so dating rests mainly on dendrochronology and on the changing fashions of brooches.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The community at Hallstatt itself was untypical of the wider, mainly agricultural, culture, since its economy exploited rich salt mines. These were extensively mined, with a peak from the 8th to the 5th centuries BCE, and the salt preserved textiles, wood, leather, shoes and miners' backpacks. Some 1,300 burials are known from the cemetery.</p><p>The culture fell into two zones. The western zone covered parts of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and its elite were buried with a sword or dagger and sometimes a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>. In the eastern zone, which included eastern Austria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, warriors were buried with an axe, helmet and breastplate, and art more often told stories.</p><p>After about 600 BCE the founding of Marseille and the arrival of Greek and Etruscan goods led to long-range trade up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> valley. Powerful local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> emerged that controlled the redistribution of luxury goods, among them the Greek Vix krater and the Greek cauldron from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hochdorf-chieftains-grave">Hochdorf</a>. At the end of the period many major centres were abandoned, and towns did not re-emerge across temperate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> until the 3rd and 2nd centuries BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Farming was the base of the economy, but metalworking was advanced and iron became more common after about 800 BCE. Settlements were mostly small, though the largest, known as princely seats, were fortified hilltop sites with workshops, elite residences and rich burials. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heuneburg">Heuneburg</a> on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mont-lassois">Mont Lassois</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> are typical, and some have been called the first cities north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>.</p><p>Rulers lay under large tumuli in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>-lined chambers with wagons and bronze vessels. Ancient DNA has been taken to show matrilineal elites linked by marriage over distances of more than 100 km, and the archaeologist Dirk Krausse has suggested that some rulers may have been sacral kings. Later Hallstatt art in the west is generally agreed to be the early period of Celtic art, and the culture is commonly associated with speakers of Proto-Celtic.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hallstatt culture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Archaeologically, the story begins with the <b>Hallstatt culture</b>, which grew out of the Late Bronze Age Urnfield culture of central Europe as iron working spread from about 800 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The complex also had a long-term influence on the <b>Hallstatt culture</b> of Central Europe, which adopted trousers from the steppe nomads.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cimmerians">Cimmerians · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>It grew out of the final phase of the <b>Hallstatt culture</b>, HaD (c. 650–450 BCE), without any definite break; the change is detected mainly through elite objects in the new style, which first appear on the western edge of the old Hallstatt region.</blockquote><a href="/culture/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…of southern Britain, with its rich barrow burials near Stonehenge from around 2000 BCE, the gold and bronze of Bronze Age Denmark and the princely graves of the <b>Hallstatt culture</b>, such as the Hochdorf Chieftain’s Grave of about 530 BCE, are widely read as the work of chiefs.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
